      "final_answer": "The sequential and parallel patterns described by the Google Cloud guide have distinct characteristics and use cases:\n\n1. **Sequential Pattern**:\n   - **Structure**: This pattern executes a series of specialized agents in a predefined, linear order. The output from one agent serves as the direct input for the next agent.\n   - **Workflow Management**: It uses a sequential workflow agent that operates on predefined logic without consulting an AI model for orchestration.\n   - **Use Case**: It is best suited for highly structured, repeatable processes where the sequence of operations does not change, such as a data processing pipeline that involves steps like data extraction, cleaning, and loading.\n   - **Efficiency**: While it can reduce latency and operational costs, it lacks flexibility due to its rigid structure, making it challenging to adapt to dynamic conditions or skip unnecessary steps.\n\n2. **Parallel Pattern**:\n   - **Structure**: In this pattern, multiple specialized subagents perform tasks or sub-tasks independently and simultaneously. The outputs of these subagents are then synthesized to produce a final response.\n   - **Workflow Management**: Similar to the sequential pattern, it uses a parallel workflow agent to manage the execution of subagents without needing an AI model for orchestration.\n   - **Use Case**: This pattern is ideal when sub-tasks can be executed concurrently, allowing for reduced latency and the gathering of diverse perspectives.\n   - **Efficiency**: While it can enhance performance by executing tasks in parallel, it may incur computational overhead due to running multiple agents simultaneously.\n\nIn summary, the sequential pattern is more rigid and structured, suitable for linear processes, while the parallel pattern offers flexibility and speed by allowing concurrent execution of tasks.",
